  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Evaluating only on MATH and/or GSM8K without addressing generalizability. EVERY paper in this survey received reviewer criticism for narrow evaluation scope. At minimum, include one non-MATH benchmark (competition math, code, science reasoning) or provide a principled argument for why math-only evaluation is sufficient for your specific contribution.",
    "Comparing reward models only via best-of-N without testing in RL or step-level search settings. 'Let's Verify Step by Step' was explicitly criticized for evaluating 'only via best-of-N, never training the generator with the PRM.' Best-of-N is necessary but insufficient — it tests the RM's ranking ability but not its utility as a training signal or search heuristic.",
    "Using non-comparable training sets when comparing supervision types. 'Let's Verify Step by Step' acknowledged that 'the large-scale ORM and PRM training sets are not directly comparable (different sizes, different sampling strategies).' Confounding data quantity/quality with supervision type is a common flaw that reviewers catch.",
    "Ignoring the compute cost of data collection when claiming efficiency. OmegaPRM and Math-Shepherd both require substantial inference compute for MC rollouts. Report total FLOPs (including data collection) in addition to training FLOPs, or reviewers will question the practical advantage.",
    "Claiming novelty for dense-reward techniques without citing classical RL precedent. A reviewer gave 'Let's Verify Step by Step' a 3/10 partly because dense reward provision is 'well-known in the RL community.' Always position PRM contributions relative to the RL literature on reward shaping, advantage estimation, and dense rewards.",
    "Using 'process supervision' and 'process reward' interchangeably when they mean different things. Process supervision is the training signal (step-level labels); process rewards are the model's predictions at inference time. PAVs showed that the right process reward (advantages) may not correspond to the naive process supervision target (step correctness).",
    "Over-relying on a single aggregation strategy for PRM scores. 'Let's Verify Step by Step' used product-of-probabilities; Math-Shepherd used minimum; Scaling Test-Time Compute found last-step scoring surprisingly effective. The optimal aggregation is task- and model-dependent — ablate this choice rather than assuming one is universally best.",
    "Presenting results only at a single compute budget. Scaling Test-Time Compute demonstrated that relative method performance can REVERSE at different compute levels (beam search dominates at low budgets, degrades at high budgets). Always show scaling curves, not just point estimates.",
    "Failing to account for the base model's contribution vs. the PRM's contribution. A strong base model with self-consistency may already capture much of what a PRM provides. Always include a self-consistency baseline (majority voting without a verifier) to demonstrate the marginal value of the PRM.",
    "Using a verifier trained on one model's outputs to verify a different model's outputs without acknowledging the distribution shift. Math-Shepherd showed that distribution-matched PRMs (trained on the target model's completions) outperform PRMs trained on a stronger model's completions (PRM800K trained on GPT-4 outputs). This generator-verifier alignment is underappreciated."
  ],

  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions. First, the 'what to predict' question is becoming the central theoretical frontier: PAVs showed that advantages outperform Q-values, GenRM showed that generative verification outperforms discriminative, and the next step is a unified theoretical framework that characterizes the optimal prediction target for step-level verifiers as a function of downstream use (search vs. RL vs. reranking) and problem structure. Expect 2026-2027 papers to formalize this via connections to information theory (what target maximizes information about solution correctness?) and optimal control (what target minimizes regret in sequential decision-making?). Second, the automation-and-scaling axis is nearing saturation for math — MC rollout estimation is well-understood, and the remaining gains from scaling data are likely incremental. The real scaling frontier is domain transfer: adapting MC-based process supervision to code (via test execution), science (via simulation), and open-ended reasoning (via LLM-as-judge oracles). The first convincing cross-domain PRM paper will be highly impactful. Third, the integration of PRMs into end-to-end training loops (not just inference-time reranking) is accelerating. Math-Shepherd's step-level PPO and PAVs' dense RL rewards are early steps, but the field is moving toward systems where the generator, verifier, and prover co-evolve — a self-play paradigm where better generators produce harder verification challenges that train better verifiers that in turn provide richer rewards for the generator. This mirrors the AlphaGo trajectory (static evaluation → MCTS → self-play) and may follow a similar path. The wildcard is whether foundation model providers (who can train at frontier scale) or academic labs (who can innovate on algorithms and formulations) will drive the next breakthroughs — the PAVs and Scaling Test-Time Compute papers suggest that algorithmic innovations (what to predict, how to allocate compute) currently offer more leverage than pure scaling, which is good news for academic researchers.",

  "meta_advice": "If you are writing a 2026 paper in this area, here is what the evidence from these 17 papers tells you to do. First, pick a reformulation over an incremental improvement — papers that redefine the prediction target (PAVs) or the verification paradigm (GenRM) consistently receive higher ratings and more citations than papers that train the same thing better. Ask 'what should the verifier predict?' before asking 'how should we train it?' Second, your easiest path to a strong contribution is the cross-domain transfer gap — every paper is on MATH/GSM8K, every reviewer complains about this, and the first paper to convincingly demonstrate automated process supervision for code generation (using test-case pass rates as the MC oracle) or scientific reasoning will fill the single most-cited limitation in the literature. Third, budget at least 40% of your experimental compute for ablations and baselines. The papers that survived hostile reviewers (Lightman et al. survived a 3/10, GenRM survived a 3/10) did so because their empirical contributions were so thorough that Area Chairs had objective grounds to overrule — comprehensive ablations are insurance against adversarial reviewing. Fourth, release something — a dataset, a trained PRM, a benchmark, a codebase. PRM800K's release was explicitly cited as an acceptance factor; OmegaPRM's 334+ citations correlate with its released annotations. Community contributions create citation gravity that pure-methods papers cannot match. Fifth, ground your contribution theoretically. Connect to advantage estimation, reward shaping, information gain, or optimal control — the RL theory community and the LLM reasoning community are converging, and papers at the intersection (PAVs connecting to Ng et al. 1999) receive disproportionate praise. Avoid the trap of the 'engineering paper' that trains a slightly better PRM on a slightly larger dataset with a slightly different loss function — that game has diminishing returns. The highest-impact contributions left on the table are: (a) a unified framework comparing discriminative PRMs, advantage-based PAVs, and generative GenRM-CoT under controlled conditions; (b) the first cross-domain PRM that works on code or science; (c) a self-play system where the generator, verifier, and prover co-evolve; and (d) a theoretical characterization of what the optimal process reward target is as a function of the downstream decision-making procedure."
}
